      }
    ],
    "safety": {
      "cautions": [
        "Stop if you feel sharp, radiating or nerve-type pain.",
        "Muscle glow colours and activation curves are illustrative, not measured EMG.",
        "Low-back pain, disc problems or flexion intolerance: start with a McGill-style curl-up, dead bug or plank; partial curl-ups gave the best abdominal challenge for the least spine compression in one model study (Axler and McGill 1997).",
        "Shoulder pain or limited overhead mobility: bend the elbows or bring the hands to the temples.",
        "Pregnancy and postpartum (diastasis recti): get individual guidance before dynamic trunk-flexion work.",
        "Osteoporosis or vertebral fracture: avoid repeated loaded spinal flexion and twisting.",
        "The athlete mesh rests on the mat; the rig collision shapes are thicker than the mesh at the back and buttocks, so floorTolerance records how far those proxy shapes sink below the mat surface."
      ],
      "modify": [
        "Neck strain: hands behind the head (standard crunch).",
        "Tight shoulders: arms angled up toward the ceiling instead of flat overhead."
      ],
      "regressions": [
        "Crunch with the hands behind the head.",
        "Crunch with the arms crossed on the chest."
      ],
      "progressions": [
        "Slow 3 s lowering with a 2 s hold.",
        "Dumbbell overhead crunch."
      ]
    },
    "muscleNotes": {
      "core": "Rectus abdominis curls the ribs toward the pelvis. Moving the arms to load the curl-up (the overhead position is the longest lever) raised activity in all the abdominal muscles studied (Crommert 2021); a plain crunch is about 53% upper and 39% lower rectus MVIC (Escamilla 2016).",
      "obliques_r": "External and internal obliques assist trunk flexion and rise with the arm load (Crommert 2021).",
      "obliques_l": "External and internal obliques assist trunk flexion and rise with the arm load (Crommert 2021).",
      "hip_flexors_r": "Stay quiet: rectus femoris is only about 6% MVIC in a crunch (Escamilla 2016). They only steady the bent legs.",
      "hip_flexors_l": "Stay quiet: rectus femoris is only about 6% MVIC in a crunch (Escamilla 2016). They only steady the bent legs.",
      "ant_deltoid_r": "Holds the straight arms up beside the ears as the trunk curls (clinical opinion).",
      "ant_deltoid_l": "Holds the straight arms up beside the ears as the trunk curls (clinical opinion).",
      "lats_r": "Lengthened overhead; they steady the arms and ribs (clinical opinion).",
      "lats_l": "Lengthened overhead; they steady the arms and ribs (clinical opinion)."
    }
  },
